  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b2.climate-policy.warmer-world",
      "level": "B2",
      "topics": ["climate-policy"],
      "title": "A warmer world",
      "text": "Scientists agree that the planet is getting warmer and that people are the main cause. When we burn fossil fuels such as coal, oil and gas, we release emissions that trap heat in the atmosphere. As a result, average temperatures have risen in almost every region, and sea levels are rising as the ice melts. Some places now suffer longer droughts, while others have more floods. Governments have tried different policies. Some have raised taxes on gasoline, and others have banned old cars from city centers. Many countries are investing in renewable energy, like wind and solar power, which produces almost no emissions. But individuals matter too. Your carbon footprint is the total amount of greenhouse gases that your lifestyle produces: how you travel, what you eat and how much you buy. Flying less, eating less meat and keeping things for longer can all make a difference. The sun still rises every morning, as it always has, but the world it shines on is changing fast.",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
